{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/837371-crossing-pantheon-through-conquest","id":837371,"title":"Crossing Pantheon Through Conquest","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-25T19:56:10-08:00","id":837371,"title":"Crossing Pantheon Through Conquest","content":"I Just realized something interesting about history,many times,when a people or a nation conquered another people or nation after a war. The conqueror did their best to assimilate the culture they conquered. After taking power the new leaders trying to make evrything that was native to the people they subjugated theirs,sometimes even acting like it always was part of the conqueror's culture in the first place. that happened a lot with gods,almost every time a people were subjugated and conquerade,the conqueror tried to impose their religion on their culture. and many times the conqueror through propaganda and manipulation,made people belive their gods,their pantheon was just a part of the conquerors pantheon. we know that happened in Scion,when servants of one of Titans made people believe their gods were not indepedents beings,but simply servants of the titan,and through the people belief,once proud gods simply become flunkies.<br /> i just thought,something pretty similar must have happened in other situtations. namely,when gods of the pantheon worshipped by a conquered people ends up becoming part of the panthoen worshiped by those who conquered his people in the first place. would that be possible in scion 1e or 2e?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-25T23:08:17-08:00","id":837401,"title":"","content":"I would expect in 2E this would be handled through the same mechanic that allows for Deities who exist in multiple Pantheons. A good example for what you are talking about specifically here would be Epona, who was a Gaulish horse Goddess and was subsumed into Roman worship when most of the other Gaulish Deities were just made into other aspects of existing Theoi.<br /> <br /> If you want some In-Myth examples of this, the Aesir-Vanir situation is pretty well known. Lots of scholarly theories about why the myth exists. I believe the most common one is exactly what you talk about here. A more Agrarian focused culture was present in Northern Europe who worshiped the Vanir and were conquered by an invading group who were more warlike, worshiping the Aesir. In the Andes there is a similar sort of idea we can see in the Huarochir\u00ed Manuscript. A Deity from the upper mountains (normally wielding cold winds and lightning) comes down and conquers a Deity from the lower slopes of a mountain (Normally more agrarian, warmth, heat sort of deal) and banishes them from the area. Normally understood to be an explanation for the herders on the upper slopes coming down, conflicting, and conquering / intermarrying with a more agrarian community from the lower slopes (the two clashing Deities ancestral Deities of these two communities).<br /> <br /> In RAW Scion 1E, there isn't really any established way to handle it. Epona is presented as a member of the Nemetondevos (Gaulish Gods), and we have the Aesir-Vanir presented in the Ragnarok book in more detail. Pretty much up to an individual Storyteller to decide how it works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-26T09:08:39-08:00","id":837605,"title":"","content":"Theres possibilities for drama here,a God would so angry if he found himself part of an pantheon that tried to hurt his people because of their manipulation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-26T21:39:50-08:00","id":837927,"title":"","content":"I'd like to imagine that if some mortals are directly trying to alter the Gods, that they would take steps to prevent this in some way. That's why they cut ties to the mortal world in the first place.<br /> <br /> Plus, there are also tales that some of the old cultures would challenge foreign deities with some manner of contest, as a way to prove who's gods were real and who's were false. Their followers could erect such competitions, invoking their deities to prove themselves with displays of godly power in order to persuade worshipers.<br /> <br /> Or, there's the possibility the gods would get quite pissed at this, and just smite them for their impudence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-27T08:53:13-08:00","id":838072,"title":"","content":"&quot;False gods&quot;, as a conception, was so rare as to be non-existent among polytheistic (e.g. Scion's bread and butter), most henotheistic or even many monolatrist societies. Subordination and syncretization and subsumption of the vanquished pantheon into that of the conqueror was much more the rule. That is, if the conqueror in question even stayed around. Notice, the Latin pantheon was subsumed into the Gallic one, even though the Gaul's kicked Rome's ass. They came, conquered, took what they wanted and left, which was a pretty common way of conducting warfare until the dominance of state-level entities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}